// Alert 3 — a SAS-authenticated request succeeded. Any SAS, from anywhere.
//
// Backs ARD-039 §3 (the account is not on the internet) and §8 Q1 (shared-key
// removal). This is the rule that catches a storage credential being *used*.
//
// ── Why "any SAS" and not "a SAS from outside the egress" ───────────────────────
//
// Until KAT-417 T-005 this rule was scoped to callers outside the cluster's
// egress, because a customer clicking their own download link presented a SAS
// from their own address and was indistinguishable from a leak. That made the
// rule's common case benign, so it shipped DISABLED — an alert whose common case
// is benign gets muted, and a muted alert is worse than no alert because it looks
// like coverage on a diagram.
//
// T-005 relays every download through the API and deletes the last thing in the
// product that could sign anything (asserted structurally by
// `backend/tests/test_download_url_scope.py`, which refuses every signing helper
// anywhere under `app/`). So there is no longer a benign case to carve out, and
// the address is no longer the interesting dimension: **nothing this product runs
// can mint a SAS**, which makes any SAS at all a fact about a human, a leaked key
// or a second system. That is a sharper rule and a simpler one — no CIDR list to
// maintain, and no way for a stale CIDR to silently widen it.
//
// ── What a hit means ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Someone minted a SAS with the account key, which means they hold the account
// key. See docs/runbooks/storage-alerts.md. The one legitimate cause is an
// operator doing it by hand — which is exactly a thing worth knowing about, and
// the runbook's "fire it on purpose" drill for this rule is that same act.
//
// If the deferred direct-upload option (KAT-416 §8 Q5) is ever taken, this rule
// fires on every upload and must be rewritten before that ships — not muted
// afterwards. Re-opening the account to a browser is a change of security
// posture, and this rule going off is the posture saying so.
//
// ── A parsing detail kept from the previous version ─────────────────────────────
//
// `CallerIpAddress` is logged as `ip:port` for IPv4, so it is split before being
// projected. It is no longer used as a filter — an address that cannot be parsed
// can no longer make this rule silently never fire — but it is the first thing a
// responder wants, so it is still in the output.
StorageBlobLogs
| where AuthenticationType == "SAS"
| where StatusCode >= 200 and StatusCode < 300
| extend CallerIp = tostring(split(CallerIpAddress, ":")[0])
| project
    TimeGenerated,
    AccountName,
    OperationName,
    CallerIp,
    CallerIpAddress,
    RequesterObjectId,
    StatusCode,
    Uri
